narrative:
## Saying 'Anything That Can Greet'

An **interface** defines a set of methods a type must have. Any type that has those methods automatically satisfies the interface — no special keyword needed.

### Defining and Using an Interface

```xiom
interface Greetable {
  fn greet() -> Str;
}

type Person = {
  name: Str;
}

fn Person.greet(self) -> Str {
  return "Hi, I'm " + self.name;
}

fn say_hello[T: Greetable](thing: &T) {
  io.println(thing.greet());
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let person = Person{ name: "Alex" };
  say_hello(&person);
  return 0;
}
```

### How It Works

1. `interface Greetable` says: 'anything that implements `greet() -> Str` satisfies me'
2. `Person` has a `greet` method, so it AUTOMATICALLY satisfies Greetable — no `implements` keyword
3. `fn say_hello[T: Greetable]` means: 'T can be any type that satisfies Greetable'
4. Inside `say_hello`, you can call `thing.greet()` because the interface GUARANTEES T has that method

### The Magic: Checked at Use Site

XIOM checks interface satisfaction when you CALL the function, not when you define the type. If you write `say_hello(&42)`, XIOM says 'Int doesn't satisfy Greetable' — it catches the error at the call site.

### Why This Beats Writing Three Functions

Without interfaces, you'd need:
```xiom
fn say_hello_to_person(p: &Person) { ... }
fn say_hello_to_robot(r: &Robot) { ... }
fn say_hello_to_pet(p: &Pet) { ... }
```

With interfaces: one function, infinite types — as long as they can greet.

